From: Escapement
Subject: [PATCH] dts: put I2C on the T-Watch Ultra's pins and add its RTC

The board profile this port ships (devkit-c1) puts i2c0 on GPIO6/7. The
T-Watch Ultra wires I2C to GPIO3 (SDA) and GPIO2 (SCL) -- measured, not
inferred. An ESP-IDF probe on those pins found six devices answering:

    0x1A CST9217 touch     0x20 XL9555 expander
    0x28 BHI260AP sensor   0x34 AXP2101 PMU
    0x51 PCF85063A RTC     0x5A DRV2605 haptic

On GPIO6/7 the kernel's bus comes up and scans empty. Every one of those
devices was unreachable for that reason alone, not for want of drivers --
`espctl i2c scan /dev/i2c-0` printed a blank grid while they were all sitting
on the other two pins.

The RTC is a separate chip, not part of the AXP2101: an NXP PCF85063A at
0x51, interrupt on GPIO1, powered from VBACKUP. It was confirmed running and
correct before this patch, from the same probe:

    CTRL1=0x00 STOP=0 TEST=0
    decoded 2026-11-09 01:39:45
    after 2 s: 01:39:47 -> COUNTING

So the time was already there and right; all that was missing was a bus.

Notes on the edits:

  * PIN(n) in this port's gpio-esp32.h takes the GPIO *number*, not an IO_MUX
    index -- uart0 uses PIN(43)/PIN(44) for U0TXD/U0RXD. Hence PIN(3)/PIN(2),
    not PIN(IOMUX_GPIO3). A wrong guess here silently does nothing.
  * The RTC uses compatible "nxp,pcf85063a" (the A variant, per the bindings
    enum). Only compatible and reg are required; the interrupt is optional but
    wired, because gpio0 is a real interrupt controller with
    "#interrupt-cells = <2>" and the alarm is wanted later.
  * No competing RTC driver exists in this kernel, so this lands as rtc0 and
    CONFIG_RTC_HCTOSYS can seed the system clock from it at boot.

--- a/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3-devkit-c1.dts
+++ b/arch/xtensa/boot/dts/esp32s3-devkit-c1.dts
@@ -16,11 +16,23 @@
 		#size-cells = <0>;
 		compatible = "i2c-gpio";
 
-		sda-gpios = <&gpio0 6 (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH | GPIO_OPEN_DRAIN)>;
-		scl-gpios = <&gpio0 7 (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH | GPIO_OPEN_DRAIN)>;
+		/* T-Watch Ultra: SDA=GPIO3, SCL=GPIO2. The devkit profile this
+		 * port ships uses 6/7, which is why the kernel bus scanned empty
+		 * while six devices were answering on the other two pins.
+		 */
+		sda-gpios = <&gpio0 3 (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH | GPIO_OPEN_DRAIN)>;
+		scl-gpios = <&gpio0 2 (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH | GPIO_OPEN_DRAIN)>;
 
 		pinctrl-0 = <&i2c0_pins>;
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
+
+		rtc@51 {
+			compatible = "nxp,pcf85063a";
+			reg = <0x51>;
+			interrupt-parent = <&gpio0>;
+			interrupts = <1 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING>;
+			wakeup-source;
+		};
 	};
 };
 
@@ -29,8 +41,8 @@
 
 	i2c0_pins: i2c0_pins {
 		pinctrl-single,pins = <
-			PIN(6) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU			/* SDA */
-			PIN(7) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU>;		/* SCL */
+			PIN(3) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU			/* SDA */
+			PIN(2) FUN0_20MA_IE_WPU>;		/* SCL */
 	};
 	spi2_pins: spi2_pins {
 		pinctrl-single,pins = <
